{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgXDPV2hY2cCCi75kr7Mf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before I draw anything, this is how I read the request.\n\nQuestion: who does what, and where does the claim change hands?\nOutcome: a CS or warehouse lead can see which team owns each step of a damaged-item refund and where the claim moves from CS to the warehouse.\nDetail level, audience and distance: Balanced, Mixed and Laptop.\nSettled by the sources: the support runbook gives the steps, the three lanes (customer, CS agent, warehouse operator) and one decision.\n* The customer reports damage with a photo through the help form.\n* The CS agent checks the photo. Clear damage is approved on the spot, and unclear damage sends the customer back to add a second photo.\n* An approved claim is queued for the warehouse, where the operator books a courier, inspects the item on arrival and releases the refund.\n* Every decision is logged in the claims table. I'll draw that table once, fed from the approval, and say in the source line that every decision writes to it.\n\nHighlight: the CS step that approves the claim and hands it to the warehouse, which is the first glance. The refund release is the goal and stays unstyled.\nLeft out: pickup skipped under €20, a pilot that is not live. The runbook says nothing about a claim CS declines, so the board shows no decline path.\nAssumed: CS and warehouse leads as the reader, and the three dial settings above. A different reader or a different place to read the board would settle either.\nStill open: none that blocks drawing.\n\nIs this the right reading of the board?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1203,"cache_read_input_tokens":86826,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1203},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","infere